You receive a raw block of text pasted from a job application form. It may contain one or several separate questions or fields, mixed with UI noise from copy-pasting a web page - widget-type labels like "Short answer", "Long answer", "Paragraph", "Multiple choice", "Dropdown", "Checkboxes", asterisks marking required fields, and so on. Your job is only to split it into individual distinct questions/fields and classify them. You do not answer any of them here.

RAW PASTED FORM TEXT
{{ raw_text }}

IMPORTANT - TELL REAL ANSWER OPTIONS APART FROM UI WIDGET LABELS
Some questions in the pasted text are immediately followed by their actual answer options - most commonly "Yes" / "No", but sometimes a short list of named choices (e.g. a dropdown's items). Those ARE real options and must be captured as "choice" type with each option listed.
Separately, some questions are followed by a widget-type label like "Short answer", "Long answer", "Paragraph text", "Long text". Those are NOT answer options - they only describe the kind of text field the form uses. Never treat them as options, and never include them in the question text itself. A question followed by one of these labels (or by nothing at all) is free text - classify it as "short_text" or "document" based on the guidance below, never as "choice".

CLASSIFICATION
For each distinct question or field, decide its answer_type:
- "choice": the question is immediately followed by 2 or more real, literal answer options (most commonly Yes/No) that the candidate must pick from.
- "short_text": a short factual or free-text answer typed directly into a form field (years of experience, availability, a short "why us" answer, salary expectations, numeric answers).
- "document": the field expects a full written document as the answer - a cover letter, a motivation letter, a longer "tell us about yourself" essay, or anything else that reads as a standalone document rather than a short field answer.
- "other": the field asks for a non-text file upload (a photo, a scanned ID, a portfolio file, a video) or anything that isn't text at all.

IMPORTANT - ONLY EXTRACT QUESTIONS THAT NEED A WRITTEN ANSWER
The candidate only wants help with questions that need a genuine written response - cover letters, "tell us about yourself" style summaries, "why do you want this role", project descriptions, or any other open-ended text field. Skip entirely: yes/no questions, simple factual fields (years of experience, availability date, expected salary, visa status), dropdown/choice questions, and anything the candidate can answer in a few words themselves. Do not output a <question> tag for any of these - just omit them.

TASK
Extract each distinct question that needs a written answer, as it was actually asked (clean up formatting noise and required-field markers like "*", but do not paraphrase or change its meaning, and never fold a widget-type label into the question text). Skip anything that is clearly not a question or field (page furniture, unrelated boilerplate), and skip short-answer/choice questions per the rule above. If the pasted text contains only one qualifying question, output only one.

OUTPUT FORMAT
Respond with this shape (no nested tags, the question text is the tag's content). If the pasted form text shows an explicit character-limit indicator for this field (e.g. a counter like "0/600" or text like "max 600 characters"), set char_limit to that number - otherwise omit the attribute entirely, never guess a number:
<question type="document" char_limit="explicit numeric limit if shown, omit otherwise">The exact question or field text</question>

Repeat <question> once per qualifying question found, in the order they appeared, with no extra text outside these tags. Use type="document" for every question - this pipeline only handles written-answer questions.